  "name": "SEROTONIN  5 HYDROXY TRYPTAMINE , SERUM",
  "code": "2842",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>1. Consumption of food that contain Serotonin like bananas, walnuts, tomatoes, pineapples, plums, gooseberries,<br>avocados, aubergines, kiwi fruits and chocolate etc. may cause false elevation of serum Serotonin levels.<br>2. Medications like Lithium, MAO Inhibitor, Methyldopa, Morphine, Reserpine, Fluoxetine etc. may affect Serotonin levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Comment&nbsp;</strong>Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine; 5-HT) is one of the most thoroughly characterized biologically active substance&nbsp;released by Carcinoid tumors. Carcinoid are neuroectodermal tumors of the gastrointestinal tract and pancreas. Midgut&nbsp;Carcinoids release large quantity of Serotonin into circulation; however tumors derived from foregut and hindgut may&nbsp;produce relatively small amount of Serotonin. When a patient strongly suspected for Carcinoid syndrome shows normal or&nbsp;borderline increase in Chromogranin A or 5-HIAA levels, documentation of elevated Serotonin concentration may help to&nbsp;establish the diagnosis. In a symptomatic patient, serum Serotonin concentration greater than 400 &mu;g/L is consistent with&nbsp;Carcinoid syndrome. To achieve maximum sensitivity in the initial diagnosis of suspected Carcinoid tumors, serum Serotonin,&nbsp;Urinary 5-HIAA, and serum Chromogranin A should be measured. The presence of functioning Carcinoid can be excluded if all&nbsp;are normal.</p>",
